/**
\defgroup distrib_weibull Weibull
\brief Continuous positive-line distribution parametrized by mean \f$m > 0\f$ and shape \f$\sigma > 0\f$, with closed cumulative functions, quantiles, and expected Fisher information.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/weibull.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::weibull_distrib "weibull_distrib", this module evaluates the Weibull log-density, its parameter derivatives to order \f$6\f$, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, cumulative functions and derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ to order \f$6\f$, quantiles, closed expected Fisher information with derivatives to order \f$4\f$, and host starting values. Every evaluator except `start_value()` is marked `LAPLACE_HD` so it runs on host CPUs and inside Kokkos kernels on the device (see \ref distrib_core "distrib_core"). Random variates come from \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes" by quantile inversion, and starting values follow \ref distrib_start "Starting values" through \ref distrib_generic "distrib_generic". On the `gcc` build against `mpmath` reference values, \f$\log f\f$ and its parameter derivatives stay within \f$45.3\f$ scaled ulp, \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ within \f$12.1\f$ scaled ulp, \f$\log F\f$ within \f$479\f$ scaled ulp, mixed derivatives within \f$137\f$ scaled ulp, and the expected information within \f$3.0 \times 10^{-16}\f$ of the natural scale.

## Classic form and parametrization

The textbook Weibull distribution (Weibull, 1951) on \f$y > 0\f$ has shape \f$k > 0\f$ and scale \f$b > 0\f$, with density \f$f(y; k, b) = (k/b)(y/b)^{k-1}\exp(-(y/b)^k)\f$ and mean \f$b\,\Gamma(1 + 1/k)\f$ (Johnson, Kotz and Balakrishnan, 1994). Setting \f$k = \sigma\f$ and \f$\log b = \log m - \log\Gamma(1 + 1/\sigma)\f$ makes the first parameter the mean \f$m = \mathbb{E}[Y] > 0\f$ at every shape \f$\sigma > 0\f$. The implemented log-density is
\f[
\ell(m, \sigma; y) = \log\sigma - \log y + \sigma\log\!\left(\frac{y}{m}\right) + \log\Gamma\!\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sigma}\right) - \exp\!\left(\sigma\log\!\left(\frac{y}{m}\right) + \log\Gamma\!\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sigma}\right)\right), \qquad y > 0.
\f]
The scale \f$b\f$ is never formed as a number, because at small \f$\sigma\f$ it underflows while \f$\log b\f$ stays finite. At \f$\sigma = 1\f$, \f$b = m\f$ and the family reduces to \ref distrib_exponential "the exponential distribution" with mean \f$m\f$.

The implementation introduces \f$x = 1/\sigma\f$, \f$\phi(x) = \log\Gamma(1 + x)/x\f$, \f$C = \log(y/m)\f$ (computed via \f$\operatorname{log1p}((y - m)/m)\f$ when \f$|y - m| < m/2\f$), \f$h = \sigma C + \phi(x)\f$, and \f$T = e^h = (y/b)^\sigma\f$, so that \f$\ell = \log\sigma - \log y + h - T\f$. At small \f$\sigma\f$ (\f$x \ge 2\f$), the pure-\f$\sigma\f$ part is grouped as \f$P(\sigma) = \log\sigma + \phi(1/\sigma)\f$ so \f$\log\sigma\f$ cancels the \f$\log x\f$ term of \f$\phi(x)\f$. Under the model, \f$T\f$ is a standard exponential random variable at every \f$(m, \sigma)\f$. This is why the expected Fisher information is closed and why the survival function is \f$e^{-T}\f$, giving \f$\log(1 - F) = -T\f$ exactly.

## Parameters, domain, and regularity

Struct `weibull_distrib` sets `kind = distrib_kind::continuous`, `n_params = 2`, and `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`. Both `m` and `sigma` have open domain \f$(0, \infty)\f$, `circular = false`, `moves_support = false`, `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`. The support is \f$(0, \infty)\f$, and `param_kinks()` and `y_kinks()` return empty sets. Member `center()` returns \f$m\f$, `log_scale()` returns \f$\log b = \log m - x\,\phi(x)\f$, and `scale()` returns the standard deviation \f$m\sqrt{\Gamma(1 + 2/\sigma)/\Gamma(1 + 1/\sigma)^2 - 1}\f$ written as \f$m\,e^{L/2}\sqrt{-\operatorname{expm1}(-L)}\f$ with \f$L = \frac{2}{\sigma}(\phi(2/\sigma) - \phi(1/\sigma))\f$ so the Gamma factors never divide or cancel. Parameter derivatives pack into `sym_tensor<2, K>`, whose component \f$c\f$ holds \f$c\f$ derivatives in \f$\sigma\f$ and \f$K - c\f$ in \f$m\f$ (see \ref distrib_core "distrib_core"). Member `start_value()` sets \f$m\f$ to the sample mean \f$\bar{y}\f$ and \f$\sigma = (s_y / \bar{y})^{-1.086}\f$ (where \f$s_y\f$ is the sample standard deviation) floored at \f$10^{-2}\f$, defaulting to \f$m = 1\f$ (if non-positive or non-finite) and \f$\sigma = 1\f$ on degenerate samples, each moved inside the open domain (\ref distrib_start "Starting values").

## Derivatives and auxiliary routes

For a multi-index \f$\alpha = (j, a, c)\f$ with \f$j\f$ derivatives in \f$y\f$, \f$a\f$ in \f$m\f$, and \f$c\f$ in \f$\sigma\f$ (\f$j + a + c \le 6\f$), each component of \f$\ell\f$ has the form
\f[
\partial^\alpha \ell = [\text{terms of }\log\sigma\text{ and }-\log y] + \partial^\alpha h - e^h R_\alpha, \qquad R_\alpha = e^{-h}\,\partial^\alpha e^h.
\f]
For pure-\f$\sigma\f$ components (\f$j = a = 0\f$, \f$c \ge 1\f$), the leading terms are \f$P^{(c)}(\sigma) + C\,\delta_{c,1}\f$; for pure-\f$y\f$ components (\f$a = c = 0\f$, \f$j \ge 1\f$), \f$(-1)^j (j - 1)!\,y^{-j}\f$ is added for \f$-\log y\f$. Here \f$R_\alpha\f$ is the complete Bell polynomial of the partials of \f$h\f$ over the set partitions of \f$\alpha\f$ (Johnson, 2002), evaluated from \f$R_0 = 1\f$ without polynomial tables by the Leibniz recurrence
\f[
R_{\alpha + u} = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha} \binom{\alpha}{\beta} h_{u + \beta} R_{\alpha - \beta},
\f]
where \f$u\f$ is a unit multi-index, \f$\binom{\alpha}{\beta}\f$ is the product of componentwise binomial coefficients, and \f$h_\alpha = \partial^\alpha h\f$. The non-zero partials of \f$h\f$ are
\f[
h_{0,a,0} = \sigma\,\frac{(-1)^a (a - 1)!}{m^a}, \qquad h_{0,a,1} = \frac{(-1)^a (a - 1)!}{m^a} \quad (a \ge 1),
\f]
\f[
h_{0,0,c} = C\,\delta_{c,1} + \frac{\mathrm{d}^c \phi(1/\sigma)}{\mathrm{d}\sigma^c} \quad (c \ge 1),
\f]
\f[
h_{j,0,0} = \sigma\,\frac{(-1)^{j-1} (j - 1)!}{y^j}, \qquad h_{j,0,1} = \frac{(-1)^{j-1} (j - 1)!}{y^j} \quad (j \ge 1).
\f]
Every mixed partial combining \f$m\f$ with \f$y\f$, or carrying two or more derivatives in \f$\sigma\f$ together with any derivative in \f$m\f$ or \f$y\f$, vanishes identically.

Derivatives of \f$\phi(1/\sigma)\f$ and \f$P(\sigma)\f$ in \f$\sigma\f$ are composed from \f$\phi^{(n)}(x)\f$ via \ref faa_di_bruno "faa_di_bruno", switching among three routes in \f$x = 1/\sigma\f$:

- **Series** for \f$x \le 1/4\f$ (\f$\sigma \ge 4\f$): \f$\phi(x) = -\gamma + \sum_{i \ge 1} (-1)^{i+1}\zeta(i + 1) x^i / (i + 1)\f$, differentiated term by term with \f$\zeta(i + 1) - 1\f$ tabulated to \f$i = 63\f$, because the direct form \f$\log\Gamma(1 + x)/x\f$ loses relative accuracy like \f$\varepsilon/x\f$ at small \f$x\f$.
- **Leibniz rule** for \f$1/4 < x < 2\f$: Leibniz's rule on \f$\log\Gamma(1 + x)\f$ and \f$1/x\f$, using \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()" and \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma()" (\ref gamma "gamma").
- **Stirling form** for \f$x \ge 2\f$ (\f$\sigma \le 1/2\f$): writing \f$\log\Gamma(1 + x) = (x + 1/2)\log x - x + \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) + \delta(x)\f$ with Stirling's error \f$\delta(x)\f$ from \ref stirling "stirling" (DLMF §5.11) gives \f$\phi(x) = \log x - 1 + q(x)/x\f$ with \f$q(x) = \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi x) + \delta(x)\f$; Leibniz's rule on \f$\log\Gamma(1 + x)\f$ loses about one digit per order above \f$x = 2\f$, whereas differentiating \f$q(x)/x\f$ leaves nothing to cancel and cancels \f$\log x\f$ against \f$\log\sigma\f$ in \f$P(\sigma)\f$.

## Cumulative functions and quantiles

For \f$y > 0\f$, `cdf()` evaluates \f$F(y) = -\operatorname{expm1}(-T)\f$, `ccdf()` evaluates \f$1 - F(y) = e^{-T}\f$, and `log_ccdf()` evaluates \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = -T\f$, whose parameter derivatives `log_ccdf_dk<K>()` equal \f$-T\,R_\alpha\f$ from the same Bell recurrence. For `log_cdf()`, \f$\log F(y)\f$ uses \f$h + \log\operatorname{exprel}(-T)\f$ via \ref laplace::math::exprel "exprel()" (\ref exprel "exprel") when \f$T < 1\f$, where \f$h = \log T\f$ remains finite even when \f$T\f$ underflows, and \f$\operatorname{log1p}(-e^{-T})\f$ when \f$T \ge 1\f$.

Parameter derivatives `log_cdf_dk<K>()` return zero when \f$T \ge 750\f$, where \f$\log F = 0\f$ to the last bit. Elsewhere, the outer function \f$G(T)\f$ has derivatives \f$G^{(r)}(T) = (-1)^{r+1} w A_{r-1}(w)/(1 - w)^r\f$ with \f$w = e^{-T}\f$ and positive Eulerian polynomials \f$A_{r-1}\f$ when \f$T \ge 1\f$, and those of \f$\log\operatorname{exprel}(-T)\f$ via \ref faa_di_bruno "faa_di_bruno" and \ref laplace::math::exprel_d1 "exprel_d1()" through \ref laplace::math::exprel_d6 "exprel_d6()" when \f$T < 1\f$. The multivariate Faà di Bruno sums \f$S_r(\alpha) = \partial^\alpha[G^{(r)}(T)]\f$ follow the recurrence
\f[
S_r(\alpha + u) = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha} \binom{\alpha}{\beta} S_{r+1}(\beta)\, T_{u + \alpha - \beta}, \qquad T_\alpha = T\,R_\alpha,
\f]
with \f$\partial^\alpha h\f$ added at \f$T < 1\f$ so every term of \f$S_0(\alpha)\f$ carries a factor \f$T\f$ and the pole never cancels by subtraction.

Quantiles never form \f$1 - p\f$: `quantile(p)` computes \f$\exp(\log b + \log(-\operatorname{log1p}(-p))/\sigma)\f$ for \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$, `quantile_from_log(log_p)` sets \f$\Lambda = -\log(1 - e^{\log p})\f$ using \f$-\log(-\operatorname{expm1}(\log p))\f$ above \f$-\log 2\f$, \f$-\operatorname{log1p}(-e^{\log p})\f$ at or below \f$-\log 2\f$, and \f$\log\Lambda = \log p\f$ below \f$-700\f$, and `quantile_from_log_upper(log_q)` computes \f$\exp(\log b + \log(-\log(1 - p))/\sigma)\f$ directly from \f$\log(1 - p) \le 0\f$.

## Expected Fisher information

Because \f$T\f$ is standard exponential, \f$\mathbb{E}[T^j (\log T)^n] = \Gamma^{(n)}(j + 1)\f$ (Johnson, Kotz and Balakrishnan, 1994), giving the closed expected information blocks
\f[
I_{mm} = \frac{\sigma^2}{m^2}, \qquad I_{m,\sigma} = \frac{D}{m}, \qquad I_{\sigma,\sigma} = \frac{D^2 + \pi^2/6}{\sigma^2}, \qquad D = \psi\!\left(1 + \frac{1}{\sigma}\right) - (1 - \gamma).
\f]
For \f$|1/\sigma - 1| > 1/4\f$, \f$D\f$ is evaluated as that single difference via \ref laplace::math::digamma "digamma()". Near \f$\sigma = 1\f$ (\f$|d| \le 1/4\f$, \f$d = 1/\sigma - 1\f$), where \f$D\f$ passes through \f$0\f$ and \f$m\f$ and \f$\sigma\f$ are orthogonal, \f$D\f$ uses the series \f$\psi(2 + d) - \psi(2) = \sum_{i \ge 1} (-1)^{i+1}(\zeta(i + 1) - 1) d^i\f$ to retain full relative accuracy. Parameter derivatives `expected_info_dk<K>()` compose \f$\psi^{(n)}(1 + 1/\sigma)\f$ with \f$1/\sigma\f$ via \ref faa_di_bruno "faa_di_bruno" and differentiate the three blocks by Leibniz's rule.

## Boundary behaviour

At \f$y \le 0\f$, `logpdf()` and `log_cdf()` return \f$-\infty\f$, `cdf()` returns \f$0.0\f$, `ccdf()` returns \f$1.0\f$, `log_ccdf()` returns \f$0.0\f$, `log_ccdf_dk<K>()` returns \f$0.0\f$ in every component, and `logpdf_dk<K>()` and `log_cdf_dk<K>()` return quiet NaN. The derivative of \f$\ell\f$ in \f$y\f$ exists at \f$y = 0\f$ only when \f$\sigma = 1\f$ (the exponential density at scale \f$m\f$), so `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J \ge 1\f$) at \f$y \le 0\f$ returns \f$-(-1)^K K!\,m^{-(K+1)}\f$ for \f$J = 1\f$ and \f$0.0\f$ for \f$J \ge 2\f$ on pure-\f$m\f$ components when \f$\sigma = 1\f$, and \f$+\infty\f$ on every other component or when \f$\sigma \ne 1\f$. At the probability endpoints, `quantile(0.0)` and `quantile_from_log_upper(0.0)` return \f$0.0\f$, `quantile(1.0)` and `quantile_from_log(0.0)` return \f$+\infty\f$, and out-of-domain inputs return quiet NaN.

## Accuracy and speed

In `tests/distrib/test_weibull.cpp`, errors against `mpmath` (The mpmath development team, 2023) references are measured in scaled ulp (see \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" and \ref accuracy_conditioned): each component with \f$a\f$ derivatives in \f$m\f$, \f$c\f$ in \f$\sigma\f$, and \f$j\f$ in \f$y\f$ is weighted by \f$m^a \sigma^c y^j\f$, divided by the ulp of the order's largest weighted component at that point and by \f$1 + \sigma|\log(y/m)|\f$ (times \f$1 + T\f$ for \f$\log F\f$, and times \f$\max(1, \sigma)\f$ at order \f$1\f$ in \f$m\f$ for pure-\f$\theta\f$ files). Table maxima are measured on `gcc` (`docs/numerics.md`).

| Quantity | Order \f$0\f$ (max / bound) | Order \f$1\f$ | Order \f$2\f$ | Order \f$3\f$ | Order \f$4\f$ | Order \f$5\f$ | Order \f$6\f$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `logpdf` | \f$45.3\f$ / \f$80\f$ | \f$7.33\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$8.01\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$12.1\f$ / \f$20\f$ | \f$9.78\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$8.79\f$ / \f$40\f$ | \f$8.09\f$ / \f$50\f$ |
| `log_cdf` | \f$2.52\f$ / \f$20\f$ | \f$4.10\f$ / \f$20\f$ | \f$13.0\f$ / \f$30\f$ | \f$9.75\f$ / \f$40\f$ | \f$110\f$ / \f$150\f$ | \f$402\f$ / \f$600\f$ | \f$479\f$ / \f$900\f$ |
| `log_ccdf` | \f$9.35\f$ / \f$30\f$ | \f$6.80\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$8.03\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$12.1\f$ / \f$20\f$ | \f$9.78\f$ / \f$25\f$ | \f$8.78\f$ / \f$40\f$ | \f$8.27\f$ / \f$50\f$ |
| `logpdf_dy_dk` | — | \f$2.53\f$ / \f$20\f$ | \f$3.66\f$ / \f$30\f$ | \f$4.81\f$ / \f$30\f$ | \f$107\f$ / \f$300\f$ | \f$137\f$ / \f$210\f$ | \f$71.1\f$ / \f$250\f$ |

Against `mpmath` expected information (`weibull_info.csv`), the three blocks stay within \f$1.8 \times 10^{-16}\f$, \f$1.6 \times 10^{-16}\f$, and \f$3.0 \times 10^{-16}\f$ of their natural scales \f$I_{mm}\f$, \f$\sqrt{I_{mm}I_{\sigma\sigma}}\f$, and \f$I_{\sigma\sigma}\f$ on `gcc` (test bound \f$3 \times 10^{-14}\f$). Every tensor is also checked against central differences, the expected information against the variance of the score, the quantile against its own round trip from a probability and from either logarithm, the shape \f$\sigma = 1\f$ against the exponential distribution, and every output on the device against the same output on the host. See `docs/numerics.md` for the full measurements.

At \f$(m, \sigma) = (2, 1.5)\f$ on `gcc`, Serial and OpenMP timings are \f$39\f$ and \f$75\f$ ns for `logpdf()`, \f$360\f$ and \f$199\f$ ns for `logpdf_d6()`, \f$341\f$ and \f$187\f$ ns for total-order-5 `logpdf_dy_dk()`, \f$176\f$ and \f$88\f$ ns for `cdf()` with both logarithms, \f$788\f$ and \f$351\f$ ns for `log_cdf_d6()`, \f$85\f$ and \f$83\f$ ns for `quantile()`, \f$7\f$ and \f$62\f$ ns for `expected_info()`, and \f$138\f$ and \f$137\f$ ns for `expected_info_d4()`, rising to \f$123\f$ and \f$110\f$ ns for `logpdf()` at \f$\sigma = 0.4\f$ due to polygamma evaluations.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/weibull.hpp>

void eval_weibull(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out, double m, double sigma) {
  const laplace::distrib::weibull_distrib d{m, sigma};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("weibull_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.log_cdf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## Members

Struct \ref laplace::distrib::weibull_distrib "weibull_distrib" implements \ref distrib_core "distrib_core" for `n_params = 2`: `m`, `sigma`, `kind`, `n_params`, `y_regularity`, `params`, `logpdf()`, `logpdf_d1()`..`logpdf_d6()`, `logpdf_dk()`, `logpdf_dy_dk()`, `expected_info()`, `expected_info_d1()`..`expected_info_d4()`, `expected_info_dk()`, `hazard_log()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `log_cdf_d1()`..`log_cdf_d6()`, `log_cdf_dk()`, `log_ccdf_d1()`..`log_ccdf_d6()`, `log_ccdf_dk()`, `log_scale()`, `quantile()`, `quantile_from_log()`, `quantile_from_log_upper()`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`, `y_kinks()`, and `start_value()`.

